xpansion.\n\nWriting column conversion utilities...\n\nFor parsing cell references, I'm using a regex pattern to match the letter-number format like A1 or AA1. Then for circular reference detection, I need to trace through dependencies when a cell is set—if the current cell appears anywhere in the dependency chain of its own formula, that's an error. After setting a cell, I'll need to find all dependent cells and trigger reactive updates through the spreadsheet.\n\nNow I'm defining the core data structures and starting to build out the tokenizer and parser for handling formulas. Writing the tokenizer... Handling operator tokens... Defining the AST node types... Writing parser methods... Writing parser logic... Writing parser logic... Writing utility functions... Writing evaluator logic... Implementing binary operators... Adding more function handlers... Writing the cell setter logic... Writing cell dependency logic... Writing the recompute and sort logic... Building the topological sort... Writing topological sort... Implementing topological sort... I need to think through a few edge cases here. For the binary addition operator, I should handle null values by treating them as zero in arithmetic operations, but concatenate if either operand is a string. On the placeholder cell issue, I'm realizing I shouldn't create actual cell entries for every dependency—instead, when a cell like B1 references A1 that doesn't exist yet, I just need to track B1 as a dependent of A1, and then when A1 is eventually set, it inherits those dependents and triggers recomputation.\n\nThe key insight is that `getCells()` shouldn't return placeholder cells, so I need a way to distinguish them. Rather than using a sentinel value or a flag, I could filter based on whether a cell was explicitly set versus created only to track dependencies.\n\nDefining the cell structure...\n\nNow I need to think through the topological sort more carefully—when a cell updates, I need to recompute everything that depends on it in the right order. Kahn's algorithm works well here: I'll identify all affected cells, calculate their in-degrees based only on dependencies within that affected set, then process cells with in-degree zero first, working through the dependency graph in layers.\n\nThere's a subtle point though—when counting a cell's in-degree, I need to count dependencies that are either in the affected set or are the starting cell itself, since those are the ones that could have changed. Let me trace through a concrete example to verify this logic works correctly. A1 is startCell → count++. So inDegree(B1) = 1? But that doesn't make sense for Kahn's - cells with in-degree 0 should be ready.\n\nOh I see the issue. My in-degree calculation is wrong. Let me reconsider.\n\nIn Kahn's algorithm, we want to process cells in dependency order. A cell should be processed only after all its \"upstream\" cells in the affected set have been processed. Here, \"upstream\" means the cells that this cell depends on.\n\nFor Kahn's in this context:\n- in-degree of a cell = number of its dependencies that are in `affected ∪ {startCell}` \n\nBut a cell B1 depends on A1 (startCell), so its in-degree is 1, meaning it can only be processed after startCell is processed. But startCell IS processed first (we call `recompute(startCell)` before the topo sort).\n\nSo the proper approach is: process startCell first outside the topo sort, then for the topo sort itself, only count dependencies within the `affected` set when calculating in-degrees. That way B1 has in-degree 0 since it only depends on A1 which is already handled, making it immediately ready. C1 then has in-degree 1 since it depends on B1 which is in affected, giving us the correct order: B1, then C1.\n\nFixing the in-degree calculation... The topological sort correctly handles the dependency case where C1 depends on both A1 and B1—when A1 changes, both B1 and C1 end up in the affected set with the right in-degrees. The fix is to remo